          ]
        ],
        "exactPaths": true,
        "allowExtraConstructs": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "why-f",
      "dimension": "interpretation",
      "kind": "choice",
      "prompt": "Which argument best justifies your choice for F?",
      "multiple": false,
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "Its indicators define the construct and need not move together; dropping one would change what F means.",
          "correct": true,
          "feedback": "Correct. These are the hallmarks of formative (composite) measurement."
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "Its indicators correlate only weakly, so reflective criteria would fail.",
          "correct": false,
          "feedback": "Low correlations are a consequence, not a justification. The choice must follow from the definition, never from which statistics look better."
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "Formative constructs give larger path coefficients.",
          "correct": false,
          "feedback": "Choosing a specification for its results is outcome-driven analysis, and it is not even generally true."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "weight-f1",
      "dimension": "numeric",
      "kind": "number",
      "prompt": "Run PLS-SEM with the corrected model and enter the outer weight of f1.",
      "answer": 0.423,
      "tolerance": 0.004,
      "feedbackCorrect": "Correct. With F composite-formative, SEM Studio estimates its weights with Mode B (regression weights).",
      "feedbackWrong": "Not the expected value. Make sure F is composite-formative (so Mode B weights are estimated) and that you read the outer weight, not the loading.",
      "derivation": {
        "source": {
          "fixture": "pls-mixed-composite"
        },
        "analysis": {
          "method": "pls-sem",
          "engine": "browser",
          "estimator": "pls-pm",
          "missingData": "listwise",
          "pls": {
            "innerWeighting": "path",
            "maxIterations": 300,
            "tolerance": 1e-07
          }
        },
        "parameterKey": "weight:c_f:i_f1"
      }
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Ask of each construct: does it cause its items, or do the items make it up?",
    "Could a store have long opening hours but poor parking? What does that imply for how f1–f4 should correlate?",
    "Friendliness, courtesy and pleasantness are three ways of saying the same thing. What does that suggest?",
    "In SEM Studio the weighting mode follows the measurement kind; weights and loadings are listed separately in the results."
  ],
  "solution": [
    {
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "F is composite-formative: its four components define convenience and need not covary. X and Y are reflective: their items are interchangeable expressions of one attribute. Paths F → Y and X → Y stay."
    },
    {
      "kind": "p",
      "text": "With F composite-formative its weights are Mode B regression weights (f1 ≈ .423, f2 ≈ .430, f3 ≈ .283, f4 ≈ .421). Declaring F reflective would make α (≈ .52) and AVE (≈ .41) appear, criteria that do not apply to an index."
    }
  ],
  "nextLesson": "validating-formative-constructs"
}
